{
  "appGroupStyleRule": {
    "id": "G1",
    "chosenStyle": "iOS-style",
    "statement": "One App Group record per family, allocated on the Developer website, claimed by the IDENTICAL literal on both platforms. The entitlement literal equals the portal record identifier exactly, on iOS and on macOS. No team prefix is added on macOS.",
    "rationale": "Apple distinguishes two App Group ID styles. An iOS-style ID begins 'group.', is allocated on the Developer website, and is authorised by a provisioning profile. A macOS-style ID begins with the Team ID and CANNOT be explicitly allocated on the Developer website. Since 21 Feb 2025 iOS-style App Group IDs are fully supported on macOS for all product types, and Xcode 16.3 and later make the iOS style the macOS default. This project is new and builds with Xcode 26.5, so it takes the current, registrable style on both platforms. A team-prefixed macOS literal would not identify the portal record this contract says it consumes, which is the defect this revision closes.",
    "source": "Apple DTS, https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/721701 — 'Starting in Feb 2025, iOS-style app group IDs are fully supported on macOS for all product types.' / 'On 21 Feb 2025 we rolled out a change to the Developer website that completes the support for iOS-style app group IDs on the Mac. Specifically, it's now possible to create a Mac provisioning profile that authorises the use of an iOS-style app group ID.' / 'With Xcode 16.3, it's now the default for macOS as well.' / on macOS-style IDs: 'It can't be explicitly allocated on the Developer website.' / 'If you're writing new code that uses app groups, use an iOS-style app group ID.'",
    "buildSetting": "Xcode's Register App Groups build setting governs this behaviour for existing projects; the generated targets are new, so the Xcode 16.3+ default applies. TASK-260715-uyju7n must not override it back to the macOS style.",
    "contingency": "OC-4"
  },
  "appGroupPurposeRule": {
    "id": "G2",
    "statement": "The App Group literal is now identical across platforms, but what the group is FOR is not.",
    "iOS": "A genuinely shared filesystem container and UserDefaults suite. The host publishes the non-secret profile snapshot; the appex reads it. Host and appex run as the same user.",
    "macOS": "NOT a shared file container. The provider runs as root, so its group container resolves under /var/root/Library/Group Containers/ while the host's resolves under the logged-in user's home. The group is retained on macOS as the sandbox-visible shared namespace that the host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz will use — including as a Mach service name prefix if that channel is XPC — and so that Ceremony C1 does not have to be repeated to add it later.",
    "prohibition": "No macOS design may depend on group-container files as a host-to-provider channel. TASK-260715-3f4lxy (profile snapshot loader) is affected and is blocked on TASK-260728-7ii1xz.",
    "evidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac: Tailscale's and Surfshark's packet-tunnel system extensions both ship com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.absolute-path.read-write entries for /private/var/root/Library/Group Containers/, which is direct evidence that a root provider's group container is not the user's."
  },
